  \draw[->,blue!70!black,thick] (1.0,2.85) -- (4.5,2.85) node[midway,above] {$I$};
\end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Series circuit with two batteries (6 V and 12 V) and two resistors ($2\,\Omega$ and $4\,\Omega$). Points P and Q mark the p.d. measurement across the $2\,\Omega$ resistor and 6 V battery.}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution.} When sources aid each other their emfs add; when they oppose, subtract. Here they aid, so the net emf is $6+12=18$ V around a total resistance $2+4=6\,\Omega$:
\[
I=\frac{\text{net emf}}{\text{total }R}=\frac{18}{6}=3.0\text{ A}.
\]
Across $PQ$, the $2\,\Omega$ resistor drops $IR=3.0\times2=6$ V while the 6 V battery raises the potential by 6 V in the opposite sense, so
\[
V_{PQ}=6-6=\boxed{0\text{ V}}.
\]
Always fix a direction round the loop first, then add emfs that push that way and subtract those that push back.
\end{exercise}

