\myskip
\textbf{Current at a junction.} Charge cannot pile up or disappear at a point where wires meet (a \emph{junction}). So in steady operation, the total current flowing \emph{into} a junction must equal the total current flowing \emph{out} of it:
\[
\boxed{\sum I_{\rm in}=\sum I_{\rm out}}.
\]
This is just charge conservation applied at a point. In a \textbf{parallel circuit} the current from the source splits between the branches and recombines afterwards, so the branch currents always add back up to the total.

\begin{center}
  \begin{circuitikz}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    \draw (-2,0) to[battery1, l=$V$] (-2,3);
    \draw (-2,3) to[short] (0,3);
    \draw (-2,0) to[short] (0,0);
    \draw (0,3) to[R, l=$R_1$, i=$I_1$] (3,3);
    \draw (0,0) to[R, l=$R_2$, i=$I_2$] (3,0);
    \draw (3,3) to[short] (4.5,3);
    \draw (3,0) to[short] (4.5,0);
    \draw (4.5,0) to[short] (4.5,3);
    \draw (0,3) to[short] (0,0);
    \node[circle,fill,inner sep=1.3pt] at (0,3) {};
    \node[circle,fill,inner sep=1.3pt] at (0,0) {};
    \node[above left] at (0,3) {A};
    \node[below left] at (0,0) {B};
    \draw[->,blue!70!black,line width=1.2pt] (-1.4,3.35) -- (-0.4,3.35) node[midway,above,font=\footnotesize] {$I$};
  \end{circuitikz}
  \captionof{figure}{At junction A the incoming current $I$ splits into $I_1$ and $I_2$; at junction B they recombine. Charge conservation gives $I=I_1+I_2$.}
\end{center}

\myskip
\textbf{Equivalent resistance in parallel.} Both branches connect the same two junctions (A and B), so they share the same p.d. $V$. Applying $I=V/R$ to each branch and using $I=I_1+I_2$,
\[
I=\frac{V}{R_1}+\frac{V}{R_2}
=V\!\left(\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}\right).
\]
Writing the whole combination as a single equivalent resistance $R_{\rm eq}=V/I$ gives
\[
\boxed{\frac{1}{R_{\rm eq}}=\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}+\cdots}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
R_{\rm eq}=\frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}\ \text{(two resistors)}.
\]
Because adding a branch gives the charge another path, $R_{\rm eq}$ is always \emph{smaller} than the smallest branch resistance.

\myskip
\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} In the circuit shown, the battery current $I_3$ splits into $I_1$ and $I_2$ through two parallel branches. State the relationship between $I_1$, $I_2$, and $I_3$, and say when the three are equal.

\textbf{Solution.} Charge is conserved at the junction, so the current in equals the current out:
\[
I_3 = I_1 + I_2.
\]
The split is equal ($I_1=I_2=\tfrac12 I_3$) only when the two branches have equal resistance. In general the lower-resistance branch carries the larger share, and each branch current is smaller than $I_3$.
\end{exercise}

