\[
I=\frac{V}{R_1}+\frac{V}{R_2}
=V\!\left(\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}\right).
\]
Writing the whole combination as a single equivalent resistance $R_{\rm eq}=V/I$ gives
\[
\boxed{\frac{1}{R_{\rm eq}}=\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}+\cdots}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
R_{\rm eq}=\frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}\ \text{(two resistors)}.
\]
Because adding a branch gives the charge another path, $R_{\rm eq}$ is always \emph{smaller} than the smallest branch resistance.

