\section{Equivalent Resistance}
\label{sec:series-parallel}

Any network of resistors can be replaced by a single \textbf{equivalent resistance} $R_{\rm eq}$ that would draw the same current from the same supply. The rule for combining resistors depends only on whether they are in series or in parallel. (Here we use $R=V/I$; this is treated in full in the Resistance and Ohm's Law section.)

\myskip
\textbf{Series: resistances add.} In a series string the \emph{same current} $I$ passes through every resistor, and the potential drops add up to the supply emf. Using $V=IR$ on each drop,
\[
\mathcal E=V_1+V_2+\cdots=IR_1+IR_2+\cdots=I\,(R_1+R_2+\cdots).
\]
So the string behaves like one resistor $R_{\rm eq}=\mathcal E/I$ with
\[
\boxed{R_{\rm eq}=R_1+R_2+\cdots}.
\]
Adding a resistor in series always \emph{increases} the total resistance.

\myskip
\textbf{Parallel: reciprocals add.} Resistors in parallel connect the \emph{same two junctions}, so they share the same p.d. $V$. The current splits between the branches and, by charge conservation at the junction, $I=I_1+I_2+\cdots$. Applying $I=V/R$ to each branch,
\[
I=\frac{V}{R_1}+\frac{V}{R_2}+\cdots
=V\!\left(\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}+\cdots\right).
\]
Writing the combination as one resistor $R_{\rm eq}=V/I$ gives
\[
\boxed{\frac{1}{R_{\rm eq}}=\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}+\cdots}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
R_{\rm eq}=\frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}\ \text{(two resistors)}.
\]
Adding a branch gives the charge another path, so $R_{\rm eq}$ is always \emph{smaller} than the smallest branch resistance.

