\textbf{Question.} A 3.0 V battery supplies a $4\,\Omega$ resistor in series with a parallel pair of $1\,\Omega$ and $2\,\Omega$. Find the current through the $1\,\Omega$ resistor.

\begin{center}
  \begin{circuitikz}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
    \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=3 V] (0,3);
    \draw (0,3) to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (2.5,3);
    \draw (2.5,3) to[short] (2.5,1);
    \draw (2.5,3) to[R, l=$1\,\Omega$] (5,3);
    \draw (5,3) to[short] (5,1);
    \draw (2.5,1) to[R, l=$2\,\Omega$] (5,1);
    \draw (5,1) to[short] (5,0);
    \draw (5,0) to[short] (0,0);
    \node[blue,above] at (0,1.5) {A};
    \node[red,above] at (2.5,3.3) {B};
    \node[green!60!black,above] at (5,2.2) {C};
  \end{circuitikz}
  \captionof{figure}{Series-parallel circuit: a $4\,\Omega$ resistor in series with a parallel pair of $1\,\Omega$ and $2\,\Omega$ across a 3.0 V battery. Nodes B and C are at the same potential (same p.d. across both branches).}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution.} Both parallel resistors connect from node B to node C, so they must have the same p.d. First reduce the parallel pair:
\[
R_p=\frac{(1)(2)}{1+2}=\frac{2}{3}\,\Omega,
\qquad R_{\rm total}=4+\frac{2}{3}=\frac{14}{3}\,\Omega.
\]
Thus $I_{\rm total}=3/(14/3)=9/14$ A. The p.d. across the parallel pair is
\[
V_p=I_{\rm total}R_p=\frac{9}{14}\cdot\frac{2}{3}=\frac{3}{7}\text{ V}.
\]
Therefore
\[
I_{1\Omega}=\frac{V_p}{1}=\boxed{0.429\text{ A}}.
\]
Check: current in the $2\,\Omega$ branch is $0.214$ A; the branch currents add to $0.643$ A $=I_{\rm total}$.
\end{exercise}

